    "q-162671": {
      "dom_id": "step-q162671",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Suppose we would like to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=x+{y}^{2}+z$ on the intersection of the surfaces $3{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2$ and $x+{z}^{2}=1$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers. Then, the corresponding Lagrange multipliers $\\lambda $ and $\\mu $ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}⟨1,\\,2y,\\,1⟩=\\lambda ⟨0,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}},\\,2z⟩+\\mu ⟨1,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}},\\,2z⟩ \\\\ 3{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2 \\\\ x+{z}^{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing expressions?",
      "worked_solution": "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x+{y}^{2}+z\n$$\n\n \n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=3{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2,\\,h(x,y,z)=x+{z}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=2 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now expand the first equation of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,\\,2y,\\,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨0,\\,6y,\\,2z⟩+\\mu ⟨1,\\,0,\\,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
